## Method and reasoning
The state for two prefixes is one plus the diagonal value if the last characters match. Otherwise skip one last character and take the better adjacent state. Keep only the previous and current rows because earlier rows are no longer needed. Use the shorter string for columns.

## Walk through the sample
One common subsequence is “bcba”: take positions 2,3,4,6 in abcbdab and 1,3,5,6 in bdcaba. The final DP cell gives 4. The rows certify optimal length even when several subsequences attain it.

## Complexity
Time O(|a||b|), space O(min(|a|,|b|)).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string a, b;
    cin >> a >> b;
    if (a.size() < b.size())
        swap(a, b);
    vector<int> prev(b.size() + 1), cur(b.size() + 1);
    for (char c : a) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= int(b.size()); ++j)
            cur[j] = c == b[j - 1] ? prev[j - 1] + 1 : max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]);
        swap(prev, cur);
    }
    cout << prev.back() << '\n';
}
~~~
